  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">A recreation center is offering special prices on its pool passes and gym
        memberships for the summer. On the first day of the offering, a family paid $96 for 4
        pool passes and 2 gym memberships. Later that day, an individual bought a pool pass for
        herself, a pool pass for a friend, and 1 gym membership. She paid $72.</para>
      <!--Question 1 -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">Write a system of equations that represents the relationships between pool passes,
            gym memberships, and the costs. Be sure to state what each variable represents.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Compare your answer: \(\left\{\begin{array}{rcl}4p+2g&amp;=&amp;96 \\
            2p+1g&amp;=&amp;72\end{array}\right.\),</para>
          <para id="para-00004">where \( p \) is the price of each pool pass and \( g \) is the price of a gym
            membership for 1 person.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Question
      2 -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">Find the price of a pool pass and the price of a gym membership by solving the
            system algebraically. Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer: The system has no solutions. For example: Solving by
            elimination or substitution leads to a false equation (such as \(0=24\)).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Q#3-->
    
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <!--QUESTION
          IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
          <para id="para-00007">Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the equations in the
            system using the Desmos tool below.</para>
        <media alt="">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
        </media>
          <para id="para-00008">After you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer:</para>
          
            <media alt="Graph of a linear system. price for a gym membership, price for a pool pass.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_09_03_01.svg"/>
            </media>
          
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Question
      4 -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00010">Then make 1 – 2 observations about your graphs.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
          <list id="list-00001">
            <item>The graphs are two parallel lines that don’t intersect. </item>
            <item>The lines have the same slope but different vertical intercepts.</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Q#5-->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00012">How do your observations about the graph of this system relate to the fact that the
            system was determined to not have a solution?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00014">Answers will vary.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
